course: dictionary
lesson: 5628
title: Limericks: funny five-line poems
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A limerick is a short, funny poem of five lines. Its rhyme pattern is AABBA: lines 1, 2 and 5 rhyme with each other, and lines 3 and 4 rhyme with each other.
explain: Limericks have a bouncy rhythm. Lines 1, 2 and 5 are longer, and lines 3 and 4 are shorter. Read one aloud and tap the beat to hear it.
explain: In your lessons you may read Edward Lear's limericks, then write your own, often starting «There once was a...». To spot the pattern, look at the last word of each line and listen for the rhymes.
explain: Limerick also has a second meaning. As a place, it is a city and county in Ireland, and it has a capital letter. «In Limerick, Aoife's mam says...» uses the place name, while «A limerick is a funny five-line poem» uses the word for the poem.
fact: A limerick is a short, funny poem of five lines.
fact: The rhyme pattern of a limerick is AABBA.
fact: «A limerick is a funny five-line poem with the rhyme pattern AABBA and a bouncy rhythm.»
fact: Limerick, with a capital L, is a city on the River Shannon in Ireland.
fact: Many limericks are silly, and lots of them start with «There once was a...».
exercise: How many lines does a limerick have? | *Five | Fourteen | Three | Four
exercise: In a limerick, which lines rhyme together? | Lines 3 and 4 only | Lines 2 and 4 only | All five lines, all the same | *Lines 1, 2 and 5, with lines 3 and 4 rhyming together
exercise: What is a common way to start a limerick? | Roses are red | Dear diary | Once upon a time, in a far land | *There once was a...
test: Which river is the city of Limerick on? | The River Clyde | The River Thames | The River Nile | *The River Shannon
test: Which lines are shorter in a limerick? | *Lines 3 and 4 | Lines 1, 2 and 5 | All lines are the same length | Lines 1 and 5
exam: What does the pattern AABBA tell you about a limerick? | *Lines 1, 2 and 5 rhyme, and lines 3 and 4 rhyme with each other | Lines 1 and 2 are the same, and lines 3 and 4 are the same | The poem has no rhyme at all | All five lines end with the same word
